  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies are a mix of sun and clouds. Isolated to widely \nscattered elevated showers/sprinkles continue over South Dakota. \nTemperatures are warming into the 80s out ahead of a cold front \nanalyzed from near Leola down to Kennebec. Behind the front, \ntemperatures have only warmed into the 70s to around 80 degrees. \nAhead of the front, winds are southerly 15 to 25 mph with some \nhigher gusts at times. Behind the front, winds are becoming \nnortherly around 20 to 30 mph with gusts over 40 mph at times. \nCurrently seeing a mid-level thermal capping inversion of +14C to \n+16C at 700hpa. It was stronger/warmer than that earlier this \nmorning, and is expected to continue to decrease/cool-off a few more \ndegrees through late this evening.\n\nThe cold front will continue to march east, departing the \nsoutheastern corner of the CWA at or after 10 PM CDT. Acting as a \nforcing mechanism, the front may have enough time to interact with \nenergy aloft while still over the CWA this evening to begin \nproducing boundary-layer (not elevated) sourced convection, once the \nmid-level thermal capping inversion has been eroded sufficiently. \nStill thinking if convection develops, the shear/instability in \nplace favors the eventual formation of rotating updrafts/supercells \nthat move east out of the CWA late this evening into the early \novernight hours.\n\nWith surface obs across the western Dakotas (post-frontal) reporting \nvisibility 6-8 miles, thinking that near surface smoke is going to \nbe enough over this CWA from early this evening through early \nTuesday morning to continue mentioning in the weather gridded \nelement of the forecast database and in the messaging. By mid-\nmorning on Tuesday, the HRRR has progged the bulk of this near \nsurface smoke to have shifted south of the CWA.\n\nWith lower heights aloft over the CWA and a decent low level CAA/dry \nair advection sequence ongoing, Tuesday and Wednesday continue to \nlook cooler than normal for (both high and low) temperature. \nEnsemble clusters analysis and deterministic GSM output continue to \nsupport a dry extended forecast with a semi-flat upper ridge \nwest/upper trof central and east CONUS holding court, such that the \nsteering flow is expected to become west-northwesterly Thursday \nthrough Monday of next week. In fact, some of the Clusters analysis \nsuggests that eventually/slowly the western CONUS upper ridge will \nintensify, leading to the warming of temperatures back into the \nmid/upper 80s to upper 90s from east to west throughout the region, \nparticularly Friday through Sunday.",
